1	# Design
2	
3	How this app is built: its stack, layout and conventions.
4	Read it with `AGENTS.md`, which has the working rules and how the app is tested.
5	
6	*The following is Angular's official best-practices rules file, included verbatim from
7	<https://angular.dev/assets/context/best-practices.md>. It is part of the Angular
8	architecture.*
9	
10	You are an expert in TypeScript, Angular, and scalable web application development. You write functional, maintainable, performant, and accessible code following Angular and TypeScript best practices.
11	
12	## TypeScript Best Practices
13	
14	- Use strict type checking
15	- Prefer type inference when the type is obvious
16	- Avoid the `any` type; use `unknown` when type is uncertain
17	
18	## Angular Best Practices
19	
20	- Always use standalone components over NgModules
21	- Must NOT set `standalone: true` inside Angular decorators. It's the default in Angular v20+.
22	- Do NOT set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` explicitly. `OnPush` is the default in Angular v22+.
23	- Use signals for state management
24	- Implement lazy loading for feature routes
25	- Do NOT use the `@HostBinding` and `@HostListener` decorators. Put host bindings inside the `host` object of the `@Component` or `@Directive` decorator instead
26	- Use `NgOptimizedImage` for all static images.
27	  - `NgOptimizedImage` does not work for inline base64 images.
28	
29	## Accessibility Requirements
30	
31	- It MUST pass all AXE checks.
32	- It MUST follow all WCAG AA minimums, including focus management, color contrast, and ARIA attributes.
33	
34	### Components
35	
36	- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility
37	- Use `input()` and `output()` functions instead of decorators
38	- Use `model()` for two-way bound properties with `[(prop)]` syntax instead of pairing `input()` with `output()`
39	- Use `computed()` for derived state
40	- Use `linkedSignal()` for state derived from multiple reactive sources that must stay synchronized
41	- Prefer inline templates for small components
42	- Prefer Signal Forms (`@angular/forms/signals`) for new forms. They are stable in Angular v22+ and provide signal-based state, type-safe field access, and schema-based validation
43	- When not using Signal Forms, prefer Reactive forms instead of Template-driven ones
44	- Do NOT use `ngClass`, use `class` bindings instead
45	- Do NOT use `ngStyle`, use `style` bindings instead
46	- Do NOT import `CommonModule`, import only the directives and pipes the template uses, such as `AsyncPipe` or `DatePipe`
47	- When using external templates/styles, use paths relative to the component TS file.
48	
49	## State Management
50	
51	- Use signals for local component state
52	- Use `computed()` for derived state
53	- Keep state transformations pure and predictable
54	- Do NOT use `mutate` on signals, use `update` or `set` instead
55	
56	## Templates
57	
58	- Keep templates simple and avoid complex logic
59	- Use native control flow (`@if`, `@for`, `@switch`) instead of `*ngIf`, `*ngFor`, `*ngSwitch`
60	- Use the async pipe to handle observables
61	- Do not assume globals like (`new Date()`) are available.
62	
63	## Services
64	
65	- Design services around a single responsibility
66	- Use the `providedIn: 'root'` option for singleton services
67	- Prefer the `@Service` decorator over `@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})` for new singleton services (Angular v22+)
68	- Use the `inject()` function instead of constructor injection
69	
70	---
71	
72	## Spring
73	
74	This is a standard Spring Boot application, built with conventional Spring MVC
75	(`@RestController` request handling, a service layer, Spring Data / JPA persistence, and Bean
76	Validation). Follow ordinary Spring conventions and idioms throughout. Spring's reference
77	documentation is the guidance for the server: Spring Boot
78	<https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/4.1/reference/> and Spring Framework
79	<https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/>.
